    "https://www.brandonu.ca/jobs/job/psac-ra-ii-research-assistant-english/": {
      "title": "PSAC – RA II – Research Assistant – English",
      "description": "PSAC – RA II – Research Assistant – English | Employment Opportunities Top Toolbar Links Webmail Campus Map Contact Us Brandon University Search Site: Primary Navigation Future Students Programs Current Students Athletics Alumni Services Brandon University Employment Opportunities PSAC – RA II – Research Assistant – Englis… PSAC – RA II – Research Assistant – English Applications are to be received by: 4:30 p.m. on October 30, 2026. Classification: Research Assistant II Employee Group: PSAC Job Title: Research Assistant Department: English, Drama, Cr. Writing , Faculty of Arts Wage or Salary: $18.73/hr SAIII OR RAII Number of Hours to Work: 125 hours per position Start Date: November 9, 2026 Number of Positions available: 2 End Date of Employment: April 30, 2026 Course title and number (if applicable): NA Date Issued: October 15, 2026 Key Duties: Perform academic research under the supervision of Dr. Deanna Smid, who is writing a book on the history of beekeeping in Manitoba. Monitor and document research status and findings. Assist with duties related to the production of articles, publications, reports and presentations. Assist in the acquisition of research materials and supplies, which will include making trips to various archives. Perform routine clerical and administrative duties to support research activities. Perform other relevant duties assigned. Qualifications/Experience: Must have an interest in historical, literary research; Must have strong organizational skills; Preference will be given to a Brandon University student studying English literature, Creative writing, History, or Geography. To Apply: Submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae to Dr. Deanna Smid at smidd@brandonu.ca before 4:30 pm, October 30, 2026. Brandon University is committed to equity, welcomes diversity, and hires on the basis of merit. All qualified individuals who may contribute to the diversification of the University, especially women, persons with disabili