    "https://www.brandonu.ca/jobs/job/2026-25-food-services-worker-part-time-27-term/": {
      "title": "2026",
      "description": "2026 – 25 – Food Services Worker, Part-Time (27%) Term | Employment Opportunities Top Toolbar Links Webmail Campus Map Contact Us Brandon University Search Site: Primary Navigation Future Students Programs Current Students Athletics Alumni Services Brandon University Employment Opportunities 2026 – 25 – Food Services Worker, Part-Time (27%… 2026 – 25 – Food Services Worker, Part-Time (27%) Term Application forms are available on the Brandon University website on Careers@BU . Completed applications, or resumés and cover letters are accepted by e-mail to hrapplications@brandonu.ca . Please quote the Job Vacancy Number in the email subject line. Applications are to be received by: 4:30 p.m. on November 2, 2026 Job Vacancy Number: 2026 – 25 Employee Group: MGEU Classification : Food Services Worker (Cashier) Department : Food Services Type of Position: Part-Time (27%), Term *See Note Below Hours of Work: 14 hours per weekend * See Note Below Wage or Salary: $22.42 per hour Start Date: November 14, 2026 Probation or Trial Period: 520 hours of service Date Issued: October 27, 2026 As a member of the Brandon University Food Service team, you will uphold high standards of quality and service as set by the Food Services Manager and Executive Chef. Duties and Responsibilities: Responsibilities include supporting daily operations by performing cashier duties; maintaining the BU Fresh cooler, retail shelves and coolers, with a focus on keeping it stocked with fresh fruit, including preparing parfaits and fruit cups; maintaining soup and toast stations; executing and setting up catering functions; and adhering to all specifications and system protocols such as temperature logs, cleaning schedules, and prep lists. Additional duties include general cleaning as outlined in the system pack; and break coverage as needed. Flexibility and attention to detail are essential to support overall food service operations. Other related duties as assigned. Required Qualifications and Experience: Successful completion of Grade 12 or equivalent, as well as related experience in food production and handling. Must have excellent customer service skills, cashier experience, and a knowledge of kitchen equipment and of cleaning chemicals. Must be able to lift up to 20 kilograms, follow oral and written instructions, and work quickly. Must present clean and tidy appearance and follow all Manitoba Health regulations concerning hair and dress. Safe Food Handling course is required. The successful candidate must work well independently and in a team environment with a strong desire to succeed. A physical demands assessment may be conducted as part of the hiring process. *NOTE* This is a part-time term position until August 31, 2027, with the possibility for yearly contract renewals, and is subject to layoff or reduced hours during periods of slow activity. This position is expected to work approximately four (4) weekends shifts within a six (6) week rotation, totaling 14 hours per weekend . Actual weekend assignments, hours of work, and shift schedules may vary based on operational and staffing requirements. This position is subject to a Child Abuse Registry Check and Criminal Record Check. The successful candidate will be responsible for any service charges incurred. These checks must be submitted in accordance with the Policy in order to maintain employment. Brandon University is committed to equity, welcomes diversity, and hires on the basis of merit. All qualified individuals who may contribute to the diversification of the University, especially women, persons with disabilities, Indigenous persons, racialized persons, and persons of all sexual orientations and genders are encouraged to apply. Canadian citizens and permanent residents are given priority. Evidence of citizenship must be provided. We are committed to providing an inclusive and barrier-free work environment. This starts with the hiring process. Should you require an accommodation during any phase of the recruitment process, contact HR at hr@brandonu.ca . All information received related to an accommodation is kept confidential. To ensure this employment opportunity is accessible to all interested individuals, this posting is available in an alternate format upon request. Employment Opportunities Employment Opportunities – Open Positions Application Form                         (not required for Faculty positions) Contact Us Human Resources Room 337, Clark Hall 270 - 18th Street Brandon, Manitoba R7A 6A9 Phone: (204) 727-9782 Fax: (204) 726-1957 Email: hr@brandonu.ca © 2014–2026 Brandon University Contact Us Emergency Info Accessibility Log in"
    },
    "https://www.brandonu.ca/jobs/job/2026-2027-winter-term-course-laboratory-based-contract-opportunities-dept-of-applied-disaster-emergency-studies-ades-faculty-of-science/": {
      "title": "2026/2027, Winter Term, Course & Laboratory Based Contract Opportunities",
      "description": "2026/2027, Winter Term, Course & Laboratory Based Contract Opportunities – Dept. of Applied Disaster & Emergency Studies (ADES) – Faculty of Science | Employment Opportunities Top Toolbar Links Webmail Campus Map Contact Us Brandon University Search Site: Primary Navigation Future Students Programs Current Students Athletics Alumni Services Brandon University Employment Opportunities 2026/2027, Winter Term, Course & Laboratory Based Contra… 2026/2027, Winter Term, Course & Laboratory Based Contract Opportunities – Dept. of Applied Disaster & Emergency Studies (ADES) – Faculty of Science The Department of Applied Disaster and Emergency Studies (ADES) is seeking Contract Academic Staff to teach the course and/or lead the lab section for the course at Brandon University for the 2026 – 2027 academic year. Winter term: Classes run from January 5 to April 9, 2027, with an exam period following. Note: This job advertisement includes both a course and a laboratory section. Applicants may apply to either section: Course (3 credit hour) or Laboratory (1.5 credit hour) and qualified applicants may apply for both positions (4.5 credit hours total). 40:362 Disaster Response Management: Course and Laboratory Course:  Section: A00 Tuesday – Slot 13 – 1:40 pm – 4:30 pm (3 credit hour course) Laboratory: Section: Z10 Thursday – Slot 14 – 1:40 pm – 4:30 pm (1.5 credit hour laboratory) Course description: 40:362 Disaster Response Management This course focuses on the theory and practice of how organizations manage their responses to emergencies and disasters. The use of incident management systems, emergency operation centres and other response techniques are considered from conceptual and practical perspectives. Disaster warning systems, evacuation planning, emergency social services, and related topics are examined. The course includes lab work in which students apply a selection of tools and techniques to become familiar with the roles of community members, government and non-government organizations and the private sector through activities in the Emergency Operations Lab (EOL). 3 class hours per week, and 3 laboratory hours per week, one term. Course Lecture Applicants: The course covers the following three 4-week modules. Applicants must indicate which module(s) they are qualified to deliver for 1 credit hour each. Applicants may apply for any or all modules. Response Coordination: Incident Management Systems, EOC management, critical infrastructure, psycho-social supports (CISM) Emergency Social Services: Evacuation, sheltering and emergency accommodation, ESS, donation/volunteer management Communications: Warning systems, crisis communication, interagency relationships, After Action Reports Qualifications for the Course Lecturer: A Master’s degree in Disaster and Emergency Management or evidence of emergency management knowledge through a related discipline is required. Applicants must demonstrate relevant expertise in emergency management. Prior teaching or training experience is considered an asset. A preference will be given to candidates willing to teach in person in Brandon. However, online delivery is an option especially for an applicant interested in sharing the course with other instructors with the department’s coordination. In your cover letter, please indicate the module(s) (above) you are qualified to teach. Applicants may seek to establish an experiential/traditional equivalency to the Master’s degree are invited to contact the Chair of the Selection Committee at the address below on how to establish equivalent academic or professional credentials. Qualifications for the Lab Instructor: A Bachelor’s degree in Disaster and Emergency Management or a related disciplines is required. Applicants must have relevant expertise in the emergency management profession. A preference will be given to candidates willing to teach in person in Brandon. Successful teaching or training experience is preferred. Applicants may seek to establish an experiential/traditional equivalency to the Bachelor’s degree and are invited to contact the Chair of the Selection Committee at the address below on how to establish equivalent academic or professional credentials. Salary : $8,132 per 3 credit hour course Start date: Winter Term begins January 5, 2027 Application deadline: by 4:30 pm, November 9, 2026 Applications must be submitted via e-mail as a single, reasonably sized PDF file . The application should include the following: a letter of application, citizenship or Canadian immigration status, curriculum vitae, copies of transcripts, the names and contact information of three references. In your cover letter, clearly specify which of the above-listed course module(s) or/and laboratory section the applicant is applying to teach. Please send the application to: Dr. Christophe LeMoine Dean of Science Brandon University Brandon, MB R7A 6A9 science@brandonu.ca We thank all applicants for their interest in applying for this position and advise that only candidates selected for interviews will be contacted. Courses will run based on sufficient enrollment and budgetary approval. Issued Oct. 27, 2026 Brandon University is committed to equity, welcomes diversity, and hires on the basis of merit. All qualified individuals who may contribute to the diversification of the University, especially women, persons with disabilities, Indigenous persons, racialized persons, and persons of all sexual orientations and genders are encouraged to apply. Canadian citizens and permanent residents are given priority. Evidence of citizenship must be provided. We are committed to providing an inclusive and barrier-free work environment. This starts with the hiring process. Should you require an accommodation during any phase of the recruitment process, contact HR at hr@brandonu.ca . All information received related to an accommodation is kept confidential. To ensure this employment opportunity is accessible to all interested individuals, this posting is available in an alternate format upon request. Employment Opportunities Employment Opportunities – Open Positions Application Form                         (not required for Faculty positions) Contact Us Human Resources Room 337, Clark Hall 270 - 18th Street Brandon, Manitoba R7A 6A9 Phone: (204) 727-9782 Fax: (204) 726-1957 Email: hr@brandonu.ca © 2014–2026 Brandon University Contact Us Emergency Info Accessibility Log in"
    },
    "https://www.brandonu.ca/jobs/job/ra-iv-research-coordination-and-mixed-methods-analysis/": {
      "title": "RA IV",
      "description": "RA IV – Research Coordination and Mixed-Methods Analysis | Employment Opportunities Top Toolbar Links Webmail Campus Map Contact Us Brandon University Search Site: Primary Navigation Future Students Programs Current Students Athletics Alumni Services Brandon University Employment Opportunities RA IV – Research Coordination and Mixed-Methods Analys… RA IV – Research Coordination and Mixed-Methods Analysis Position Vacancy Applications are to be received by: 4:30 p.m. on November 5, 2026 Classification: Research Assistant IV Employee Group: PSAC Job Title: Research Coordination and Mixed-Methods Analysis Department: CREAMS Research Lab (Culture & Regulation of Learning for Engagement, Academic Motivation, and Success) Wage or Salary: $30.70/hr Research Assistant IV (RAIV) Number of Hours to Work: 160 hours total, with flexible scheduling based on research needs. Approximately 5 hours per week on average, with additional hours during periods of intensive research activity, subject to the total authorized hours. Start Date: November 9, 2026 (Flexible) Number of Positions available: 1 End Date of Employment: June 30, 2027 Course title and number (if applicable): N/A Date Issued: Oct. 28, 2026 Qualifications/Experience: A Master’s degree in Education, Educational Psychology, Educational Measurement and Evaluation, Psychology, or a closely related discipline, or equivalent advanced research training and experience.- Demonstrated academic research experience, particularly in mixed-methods research, including data collection, management, analysis, and interpretation. Knowledge of research methodologies, ethics, confidentiality, and handling sensitive research information. Strong organizational, analytical, interpersonal, and written communication skills. Ability to work independently, exercise sound judgment, manage priorities, and collaborate effectively with a research team. Preferred qualifications and experience- Experience coordinating research projects or mentoring junior researchers.- Familiarity with educational research relating to self-regulated learning, culturally responsive teaching, student motivation, engagement, inclusion, or belonging.- Experience using qualitative and quantitative analysis software such as NVivo, ATLAS.ti, SPSS, or R.- Experience preparing academic manuscripts, conference presentations, research reports, or grant applications.- Experience conducting research involving teachers, high school students, university students, or culturally diverse populations. Key Duties: 1. Support the monitoring of multiple research projects examining culturally responsive teaching, self-regulated learning, motivation, engagement, and belonging in educational contexts. 2.Provide methodological support for quantitative, qualitative, and mixed-methods research, and advanced data analysis.3.Organize, securely manage, analyze, and document research data in accordance with approved ethics protocols and confidentiality requirements.4. Contribute to literature reviews, research reports, conference presentations, and scholarly manuscripts.5. Assist in organizing research team meetings and supporting CREAMS Research Lab activities.6. Assist in identifying and securing research funding and perform other related duties. This position offers flexible work arrangements, including the possibility of remote or hybrid work, depending on the successful candidate’s location, project requirements, and university policies. To Apply: Send your cover letter, CV with names of two references, and TCPS-2: CORE certificate all in one PDF to Dr. Aloy Anyichie, anyichiea@brandonu.ca Brandon University is committed to equity, welcomes diversity, and hires on the basis of merit. All qualified individuals who may contribute to the diversification of the University, especially women, persons with disabilities, Indigenous persons, racialized persons, and persons of all sexual orientations and genders are encouraged to apply. Canadian citizens and permanent residents are given priority. Evidence of citizenship must be provided. We are committed to providing an inclusive and barrier-free work environment. This starts with the hiring process. If you require an accommodation during any phase of the evaluation process, please advise HR at hr@brandonu.ca. All information received related to an accommodation is kept confidential. To ensure this employment opportunity is accessible to all interested individuals, this posting is available in an alternate format upon request. 10.2026 Employment Opportunities Employment Opportunities – Open Positions Application Form                         (not required for Faculty positions) Contact Us Human Resources Room 337, Clark Hall 270 - 18th Street Brandon, Manitoba R7A 6A9 Phone: (204) 727-9782 Fax: (204) 726-1957 Email: hr@brandonu.ca © 2014–2026 Brandon University Contact Us Emergency Info Accessibility Log in"
    },
    "https://www.brandonu.ca/jobs/job/ra-iii-culturally-responsive-teaching-and-self-regulated-learning-in-high-school-study/": {
      "title": "RA III [Culturally Responsive Teaching and Self-Regulated Learning in High School Study]",
      "description": "RA III [Culturally Responsive Teaching and Self-Regulated Learning in High School Study] | Employment Opportunities Top Toolbar Links Webmail Campus Map Contact Us Brandon University Search Site: Primary Navigation Future Students Programs Current Students Athletics Alumni Services Brandon University Employment Opportunities RA III [Culturally Responsive Teaching and Self-Regulated Le… RA III [Culturally Responsive Teaching and Self-Regulated Learning in High School Study] Position Vacancy Applications are to be received by: 4:30 p.m. on November 5, 2026 Classification: Research Assistant III Employee Group: PSAC Job Title: RA III [Culturally Responsive Teaching and Self-Regulated Learning in High School Study] Department: CREAMS Research Lab (Culture & Regulation of Learning for Engagement, Academic Motivation, and Success) Wage or Salary: $22.01/hr SAIV OR RAIII Number of Hours to Work: 260 hours total, with flexible scheduling based on research activities and project needs Start Date: November 9, 2026 (Flexible) Number of Positions available: 1 End Date of Employment: June 30, 2027 Course title and number (if applicable): N/A Date Issued: Oct. 28, 2026 Qualifications/Experience: Required: Enrollment in, or completion of, an undergraduate or graduate degree in Education, Educational Psychology, Psychology, Sociology, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated knowledge of educational research methods (e.g.,qualitative and/or quantitative approaches).Strong organizational, analytical, interpersonal, and written communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively, manage assigned research tasks, and meet deadlines. Ability to communicate professionally and respectfully with teachers, students, and research collaborators. Demonstrated commitment to research ethics, confidentiality, and responsible handling of research data. Proficiency in standard computer applications, including Microsoft Word, Excel, and related research tools. Preferred Qualifications and Experience: Previous experience assisting with academic research, particularly surveys, interviews, qualitative coding, and quantitative data analysis (e.g., correlation). Familiarity with culturally responsive teaching, self-regulated learning, student motivation, engagement, or educational psychology. Experience working with high school teachers, students, or educational institutions. Familiarity with qualitative or quantitative analysis software such as NVivo, ATLAS.ti, SPSS, or R. Experience conducting literature reviews, and knowledge of teacher professional development, classroom observation, or instructional implementation research. Key Duties: Recruitment (e.g., students and teachers), data collection (e.g., survey, conducting interviews, and collecting relevant instructional documents) and analysis using appropriate qualitative and quantitative research methods. Assist in examining culturally responsive teaching and self-regulated learning practices, including analyzing lesson plans and instructional materials in relation to the study’s conceptual framework. Support the planning, coordination, and documentation of teacher learning team meetings, professional development activities, classroom implementation, and observations. Conduct literature review and prepare summaries relating to culturally responsive teaching, self-regulated learning, student motivation, engagement, and teacher professional development. Be able to work independently and collaboratively with other RAs, and participate in relevant CREAMS research lab/project meetings. Perform other related assigned duties. To Apply: Send your cover letter, CV with names of two references, and TCPS-2: CORE certificate all in one PDF to Dr. Aloy Anyichie, anyichiea@brandonu.ca Brandon University is committed to equity, welcomes diversity, and hires on the basis of merit. All qualified individuals who may contribute to the diversification of the University, especially women, persons with disabilities, Indigenous persons, racialized persons, and persons of all sexual orientations and genders are encouraged to apply. Canadian citizens and permanent residents are given priority. Evidence of citizenship must be provided. We are committed to providing an inclusive and barrier-free work environment. This starts with the hiring process. If you require an accommodation during any phase of the evaluation process, please advise HR at hr@brandonu.ca. All information received related to an accommodation is kept confidential. To ensure this employment opportunity is accessible to all interested individuals, this posting is available in an alternate format upon request. 10.2026 Employment Opportunities Employment Opportunities – Open Positions Application Form                         (not required for Faculty positions) Contact Us Human Resources Room 337, Clark Hall 270 - 18th Street Brandon, Manitoba R7A 6A9 Phone: (204) 727-9782 Fax: (204) 726-1957 Email: hr@brandonu.ca © 2014–2026 Brandon University Contact Us Emergency Info Accessibility Log in"
    },
    "https://www.brandonu.ca/jobs/job/ra-ii-international-students-experiences-motivation-engagement-and-belonging-study/": {
      "title": "RA II [International Students’ Experiences, Motivation, Engagement, and Belonging Study]",
      "description": "RA II [International Students’ Experiences, Motivation, Engagement, and Belonging Study] | Employment Opportunities Top Toolbar Links Webmail Campus Map Contact Us Brandon University Search Site: Primary Navigation Future Students Programs Current Students Athletics Alumni Services Brandon University Employment Opportunities RA II [International Students’ Experiences, Motivation… RA II [International Students’ Experiences, Motivation, Engagement, and Belonging Study] Position Vacancy Applications are to be received by: 4:30 p.m. on November 5, 2026 Classification: Research Assistant II Employee Group: PSAC Job Title: RA II [International Students’ Experiences, Motivation, Engagement, and Belonging Study] Department: CREAMS Research Lab (Culture & Regulation of Learning for Engagement, Academic Motivation, and Success) Wage or Salary: $18.73/hr SAIII OR RAII Number of Hours to Work: 80 hours total, with flexible scheduling based on research needs. Start Date: November 9, 2026 Number of Positions available: 1 End Date of Employment: June 30, 2027 Course title and number (if applicable): N/A Date Issued: Oct. 28, 2026 Qualifications/Experience: Required: Enrollment in an undergraduate program in Education, Psychology, Sociology, Social Work, International Studies, or a related discipline. Strong organizational, interpersonal, and written communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively, follow instructions, and meet deadlines. Willingness to learn research methods, data management, and qualitative analysis techniques. Demonstrated commitment to confidentiality, cultural sensitivity, and ethical research practices. Preferred Qualifications and Experience: Experience as an international student or demonstrated knowledge of international students’ experiences in Canadian higher education. Interest in culturally responsive teaching, self-regulated learning, student belonging, motivation, and engagement. Previous involvement in academic research, research-related volunteer activities, or student engagement initiatives. Experience working with culturally diverse student communities, including African, Black, and Caribbean students. Familiarity with literature searches, survey research, interviews, or qualitative data analysis. The successful candidate will participate in scheduled project meetings, relevant CREAMS Research Lab activities, and research training sessions as required, within authorized paid working hours. Key Duties: Under the supervision of the Principal Investigator, the successful candidate will:Assist with participant recruitment, survey administration, interview scheduling, and research communications involving international students. Transcribe interviews, organize and clean survey data, and maintain accurate research records. Support preliminary coding and analysis of interview transcripts, survey responses, and related research materials. Conduct literature searches and prepare summaries on culturally responsive teaching, self-regulated learning, international student experiences, belonging, motivation, and engagement. Assist with approved research activities involving African, Black, and Caribbean students, including focus-group preparation, data organization, and research reporting. Participate in relevant CREAMS Research Lab meetings and research training while maintaining ethics protocols. Perform other related assigned duties. To Apply: Send your cover letter, CV with names of two references, and TCPS-2: CORE certificate all in one PDF to Dr. Aloy Anyichie, anyichiea@brandonu.ca Brandon University is committed to equity, welcomes diversity, and hires on the basis of merit. All qualified individuals who may contribute to the diversification of the University, especially women, persons with disabilities, Indigenous persons, racialized persons, and persons of all sexual orientations and genders are encouraged to apply. Canadian citizens and permanent residents are given priority. Evidence of citizenship must be provided. We are committed to providing an inclusive and barrier-free work environment. This starts with the hiring process. If you require an accommodation during any phase of the evaluation process, please advise HR at hr@brandonu.ca. All information received related to an accommodation is kept confidential. To ensure this employment opportunity is accessible to all interested individuals, this posting is available in an alternate format upon request. 10.2026 Employment Opportunities Employment Opportunities – Open Positions Application Form                         (not required for Faculty positions) Contact Us Human Resources Room 337, Clark Hall 270 - 18th Street Brandon, Manitoba R7A 6A9 Phone: (204) 727-9782 Fax: (204) 726-1957 Email: hr@brandonu.ca © 2014–2026 Brandon University Contact Us Emergency Info Accessibility Log in"
    },
